        { id: "s4", label: "4. Revenue From the Sale of Goods — Recognition Criteria" },
        { id: "s5", label: "5. Revenue From Services — Percentage of Completion or Completed Contract" },
        { id: "s6", label: "6. Interest, Royalties, and Dividends" },
        { id: "s7", label: "7. Why ASPE Retained the Older Model" },
        { id: "s8", label: "8. Worked Example — Recognizing Revenue on the Sale of Goods" },
        { id: "s9", label: "9. Worked Example — Percentage of Completion for a Service Contract" },
        { id: "s10", label: "10. Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s11", label: "11. Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates" },
        { id: "s12", label: "12. Relationship to IFRS 15" },
        { id: "s13", label: "13. Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s14", label: "14. Related Standards" },
        { id: "s15", label: "15. Glossary" }
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">
</div>

<div class="callout danger" style="max-width:900px;margin:0 auto 20px;text-align:center;">ANOTHER MAJOR DIVERGENCE FROM THE GLOBAL CONVERGENCE TREND: ASPE has not adopted the 5-step, control-based revenue recognition model that IFRS 15, ASC 606, and the 2017-revised ASBE No. 14 all converged around. Section 3400 retains the older "risk and rewards of ownership" framework, closer to the pre-2018 IAS 18 approach — standing alongside Section 3065 (Leases) as one of the most significant structural divergences in this entire poster library.</div>



<div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">One of the most significant structural divergences in this entire library</div><div class="principle">ASPE Section 3400 governs REVENUE recognition — GENUINELY distinctively, RETAINING the OLDER "risks AND rewards OF ownership" framework, RATHER than the NEWER, control-BASED five-STEP model VIRTUALLY every OTHER framework COVERED throughout THIS poster LIBRARY has ADOPTED (IFRS 15, ASC 606, and THE 2017-revised ASBE No. 14 — see the COMPANION posters). THIS stands ALONGSIDE Section 3065 (LEASES) as ONE of the MOST significant STRUCTURAL divergences FROM the GLOBAL convergence TREND this ENTIRE library has DOCUMENTED (Section 3).</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Sale of goods, rendering of services, and use of enterprise resources by others</div><div class="principle">This Section applies to REVENUE arising FROM the SALE of GOODS, the RENDERING of SERVICES, and the USE by OTHERS of ENTERPRISE resources YIELDING interest, ROYALTIES, or DIVIDENDS.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>THE CENTRAL DIVERGENCE — Risk-and-Rewards, Not the 5-Step Control Model</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">A fundamentally different conceptual foundation</div><div class="text-supporting">RATHER than IDENTIFYING distinct PERFORMANCE obligations and RECOGNIZING revenue AS each is SATISFIED through the TRANSFER of CONTROL (the FIVE-step model UNDERPINNING IFRS 15, ASC 606, and THE modern ASBE No. 14), ASPE recognizes REVENUE based ON the TRANSFER of the SIGNIFICANT risks AND rewards of OWNERSHIP — a FRAMEWORK conceptually CLOSER to the PRE-2018 IAS 18 approach THAT IFRS 15 ITSELF superseded internationally. THIS is a GENUINELY fundamental, CONCEPTUALLY different STARTING point, RATHER than a MERELY mechanical VARIATION on the SAME underlying MODEL.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Revenue From the Sale of Goods — Recognition Criteria</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Four cumulative conditions</div><div class="check-list"><span>The significant risks and rewards of ownership have transferred to the buyer</span><span>The seller retains neither continuing managerial involvement nor effective control over the goods sold</span><span>The amount of revenue can be measured reliably</span><span>Collection of the consideration is reasonably assured</span></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Revenue From Services — Percentage of Completion or Completed Contract</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">A genuine policy choice between two older methods</div><div class="text-supporting">FOR revenue FROM services (or LONG-term contracts), an ENTERPRISE elects EITHER the PERCENTAGE-of-completion METHOD (RECOGNIZING revenue PROGRESSIVELY as the SERVICE is PERFORMED, WHERE the OUTCOME can BE reliably ESTIMATED) OR the COMPLETED-contract method (DEFERRING all REVENUE recognition UNTIL the SERVICE is SUBSTANTIALLY complete) — BOTH genuinely OLDER methods, DISTINCT from the SINGLE, unified PERFORMANCE-obligation satisfaction APPROACH IFRS 15 and ASC 606 REQUIRE internationally.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Interest, Royalties, and Dividends</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Specific recognition bases for passive income streams</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Income type</span><span>Recognition basis</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Interest</span><span>A TIME-proportion BASIS, taking INTO account the EFFECTIVE yield ON the underlying ASSET</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Royalties</span><span>ON an ACCRUAL basis, IN accordance WITH the SUBSTANCE of the RELEVANT agreement</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Dividends</span><span>WHEN the SHAREHOLDER'S right TO receive PAYMENT is ESTABLISHED</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Why ASPE Retained the Older Model</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Weighing implementation cost against limited incremental benefit for private-enterprise users</div><div class="text-supporting">THE global SHIFT to the FIVE-step, control-BASED revenue MODEL was LARGELY driven BY complex, MULTI-element arrangements INCREASINGLY common AMONG public, TECHNOLOGY-oriented, and SERVICE-heavy enterprises — WHERE identifying DISTINCT performance OBLIGATIONS and ALLOCATING a TRANSACTION price MEANINGFULLY improved COMPARABILITY and TRANSPARENCY for CAPITAL markets INVESTORS. FOR MANY private enterprises, WHOSE revenue ARRANGEMENTS are OFTEN comparatively SIMPLER, and WHOSE primary USERS have DIFFERENT informational NEEDS, ASPE'S standard-SETTERS concluded the SUBSTANTIAL implementation COST of adopting the NEWER model WOULD likely EXCEED its INCREMENTAL benefit — CONSISTENT with the SAME cost-BENEFIT reasoning UNDERLYING Section 3065'S retained LEASE model (see the COMPANION poster).</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Worked Example — Recognizing Revenue on the Sale of Goods</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Revenue recognized upon transfer of risks and rewards</div>
<div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A wholesale product shipment</div><div class="wk-body">A distributor ships $85,000 of product to a customer under standard FOB shipping point terms, meaning risk of loss transfers to the buyer upon shipment. The customer has good credit, and collection is reasonably assured.</div><div class="wk-answer">The COMPANY recognizes the FULL <strong>$85,000</strong> of revenue UPON shipment — the SIGNIFICANT risks AND rewards of OWNERSHIP have TRANSFERRED, the COMPANY retains NO continuing MANAGERIAL involvement, revenue CAN be measured RELIABLY, and COLLECTION is REASONABLY assured.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Worked Example — Percentage of Completion for a Service Contract</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Progressive revenue recognition based on completion progress</div>
<div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A multi-year construction contract</div><div class="wk-body">A construction company has a $6,000,000 contract, electing the percentage-of-completion method. By year end, the company has incurred $2,400,000 of the estimated $4,000,000 total contract costs.</div><div class="wk-answer">PERCENTAGE complete = $2,400,000 ÷ $4,000,000 = 60% — the COMPANY recognizes $6,000,000 × 60% = <strong>$3,600,000</strong> of REVENUE for the PERIOD, PROGRESSIVELY reflecting the CONTRACT'S completion STATUS.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What Section 3400 requires</div><div class="check-list"><span>Accounting policies adopted for revenue recognition, including the method used for revenue from services</span><span>Amount of each significant category of revenue recognized during the period</span><span>Amount of revenue arising from exchanges of goods or services</span></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgment matters most</div>
<div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Whether the significant risks and rewards of ownership have genuinely transferred</span><span>Whether collection is reasonably assured</span><span>Choosing between percentage-of-completion and completed-contract methods for services</span></div></div>
<div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Percentage of completion, and total estimated contract costs, under the percentage-of-completion method</span></div></div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Relationship to IFRS 15</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — ASPE Section 3400 vs IFRS 15</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">ASPE Section 3400</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IFRS 15</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Core recognition model</span><span>Risk-and-rewards of ownership</span><span>Transfer of control, five-step performance obligation model</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Service revenue methods</span><span>Percentage-of-completion OR completed-contract</span><span>Progressive recognition upon satisfaction of performance obligations</span></div>
</div>
<div class="note">ONE of the MOST significant, CONCEPTUALLY fundamental DIVERGENCES this ENTIRE poster LIBRARY has DOCUMENTED — ALONGSIDE Section 3065 (LEASES), ASPE'S retained REVENUE model REPRESENTS a GENUINE, deliberate DEPARTURE from the GLOBAL convergence TREND toward control-BASED revenue RECOGNITION.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where every revenue event lands</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Sale of goods revenue</span><span><span class="badge is">IS</span></span><span>Recognized upon transfer of risks and rewards</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Percentage-of-completion revenue</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge is">IS</span></span><span>Recognized progressively over the contract term</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Section</span><span>Connection</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Section 3065</span><span>Leases — the other major standard where ASPE retains an older, pre-convergence model rather than a modern global approach</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Section 1000</span><span>Financial Statement Concepts — supplies the reliability and conservatism concepts underlying this Section's recognition criteria</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cn span2">
<div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Risks and rewards of ownership</div><div class="glossary-def">The transfer of the significant benefits and burdens of owning an asset, the central criterion for recognizing revenue on goods under ASPE.</div></div>
<div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Percentage-of-completion method</div><div class="glossary-def">A method recognizing service or long-term contract revenue progressively based on the contract's measured stage of completion.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="legend"><div class="ltitle">Financial statement key (ASPE terminology)</div><div class="lrow"><div class="litem"><span class="badge bs">BS</span> Balance Sheet </div><div class="litem"><span class="badge is">IS</span> Income Statement </div><div class="litem"><span class="badge sre">SRE</span> Statement of Retained Earnings / Statement of Changes in Equity </div><div class="litem"><span class="badge scf">SCF</span> Cash Flow Statement</div></div></div>


</div>
  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.aspe3400 || []} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>
